<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Terai</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Terai is a belt of lowland plains, marshes and forest along the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a>, mostly in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and the adjoining parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Once malarial and thinly settled, it was home to the Shakya and Koliya republics and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lumbini">Lumbini</a>, the Buddha's birthplace. Gorkha expansion there led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-nepalese-war">Anglo-Nepalese War</a> of 1814–1816, and it is now Nepal's main farming region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/terai.jpg" alt="Terai" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Barunkhanal · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Terai_nepal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Terai lies south of the Sivalik Hills and north of the Indo-Gangetic Plain. It is a land of tall grasslands, scrub <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">savannah</a>, sal forest and clay-rich swamps. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>'s Terai covers about 23.1 per cent of the country's land at an elevation between 67 and 300 metres, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> the region spreads eastward from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>. Large rivers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, Karnali, Narayani and Kosi have built alluvial fans across it. The name comes from a word meaning low, marshy ground at the foot of a watershed.</p><p>The Inner Terai consists of five elongated valleys between the lower Himalayan range and the Sivalik Hills, among them the Dang and Chitwan valleys. They were agriculturally productive but extremely malarial, and under the Rana dynasty some stretches were kept forested by decree as a defensive belt called Char Kose Jhadi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Muslim invasion of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the 14th century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> nobles and their entourage migrated to the Himalayan foothills and gained control from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> to the eastern Terai over the following three centuries. By the 16th century the rulers of Palpa and Makwanpur controlled the mid-western Terai, and by the 17th the eastern Terai too. The Shah dynasty conquered the eastern Nepal Terai in the 1770s, and Bahadur Shah conquered the Tulsipur State in 1785. From at least 1786 the Shah rulers appointed officers there to levy taxes and hunt game; at the end of the 18th century 200 to 300 elephants were caught each year.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-nepalese-war">Anglo-Nepalese War</a>, the British annexed the western Terai when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sugauli">Sugauli Treaty</a> was ratified. They returned part of it, today's Kanchanpur, Kailali, Banke and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardiya">Bardiya</a> districts, in 1860, as a reward for Nepal's aid in the Indian Rebellion of 1857. The Indian Terai remained largely uninhabited until the end of the 19th century because of its marshes, jungle and malaria, and heavy logging of its sal forest began in the 1920s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Malaria was eradicated with DDT in the mid-1950s, after which people from the hills moved onto the plains, and about 16,000 Tibetan refugees settled in the Nepal Terai in 1959 and 1960. The Tharu and Dhimal are its indigenous peoples, and more than 120 ethnic groups and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> live there. The Terai is the most productive region of Nepal, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, wheat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane among its crops. Chitwan National Park was established in 1973, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> has been grown in Jhapa District since 1960.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Terai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A small British expedition under Captain George Kinloch, sent in 1767 to help the Newar king Jaya Prakash Malla, had already failed in the <b>Terai</b> lowlands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>One branch took the hill fort of Makwanpur and the adjacent lowlands of the <b>Terai</b>, controlling the main route from Bihar to the Kathmandu Valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-makwanpur">Kingdom of Makwanpur</a></li><li><blockquote>The Koliyas lived east of the Shakya republic, across the Rohini river in the <b>Terai</b> of modern Nepal and India.</blockquote><a href="/state/koliya-republic">Koliya republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The Shakyas were a clan claiming descent from the Ikshvaku kings, settled in the <b>Terai</b> on the border of modern Nepal and India.</blockquote><a href="/state/shakya-republic">Shakya republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom that grew there lay in what is now northern Bihar and the <b>Terai</b> of Nepal, and it was one of the easternmost Vedic states.</blockquote><a href="/state/videha">Videha</a></li></ul></section>
